---
import Callout from "../../guide/Callout.astro";
import GuidePage from "../../guide/GuidePage.astro";
import Path from "../../guide/Path.astro";
import PlatformAnswers from "../../guide/PlatformAnswers.astro";
import PlatformLabel from "../../guide/PlatformLabel.astro";
import Task from "../../guide/Task.astro";
import { leavesSite, REPOSITORY, sitePath } from "../../site/links.ts";
---

<GuidePage manual="docs" slug="troubleshooting" lead="The problems people run into most, and what fixes them.">
  <Task id="verify" title="A source asks you to prove you're human">
    <p>Sites behind Cloudflare and similar checks fail with <strong>Failed to bypass Cloudflare</strong> or an HTTP error such as 403.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Open the title, or the source's error screen, and select <strong>WebView</strong> (or <strong>Open in WebView</strong>). A title
        that fails to refresh offers <strong>WebView</strong> with the error, and so does a chapter the book reader couldn't get.
      </li>
      <li>
        Complete the check in the page that opens, then go back. Select <strong>Retry</strong> on the source's error screen, or refresh
        the title: pull it down, or press F5.
      </li>
      <li>
        Still failing? Under <Path>Settings › Advanced</Path>, select <strong>Clear cookies</strong>, or <strong>Clear WebView data</strong>, and
        try again.
      </li>
      <li>
        Some sites block Katari's browser signature. Changing <strong>Default user agent string</strong> on the same screen, then restarting
        Katari, can help.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <PlatformAnswers>
      <p slot="android">Keep Android System WebView up to date. An old one fails these checks more often.</p>
      <p slot="linux">The web view comes with Katari, so updating Katari keeps it up to date.</p>
    </PlatformAnswers>
  </Task>

  <Task id="source-errors" title="A source shows an error">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>HTTP 429</strong>: the site is limiting how often you ask. Wait a while, and check fewer titles at once.</li>
      <li>Other <strong>HTTP</strong> codes: open the site in <strong>WebView</strong> to see what it says.</li>
      <li><strong>No Internet connection</strong> or <strong>Couldn't reach</strong> the site: check the connection, or try <strong>DNS over HTTPS (DoH)</strong> under <Path>Settings › Advanced</Path>.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Source not found</strong>: its extension is missing. Select the source's name on the title's page to install it from a store
        that lists it, or to migrate the title.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The source stopped responding</strong>, <strong>ran out of memory on this page</strong> or <strong>couldn't be
        loaded</strong>: an LNReader plugin failed. Try again later; if it keeps failing, the plugin may need an update.
      </li>
      <li>
        <PlatformLabel platform="Linux" lead /><strong>This source needs …, which Katari doesn't have on desktop yet</strong>: the source
        uses a part of Android the Linux app doesn't have yet. Use it on Android for now.
      </li>
      <li>
        A source still fails after you changed its settings or logged in: open the extension under <Path>Browse › Extensions</Path> and
        choose <Path>⋮ › Clear data</Path>. It deletes the extension's settings, logins and saved data in the profile you're using.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Task>

  <Task id="extensions" title="An extension doesn't show up">
    <p>
      Under <Path>Browse › Extensions</Path>, extensions that can't be used are listed under <strong>Not loaded</strong>, labelled with why.
      <a href={sitePath("docs/extension-compatibility/")}>Extension compatibility</a> explains each label and lists the extension versions
      Katari loads.
    </p>
    <p>
      An installed extension marked <strong>Orphaned</strong> still works, but no store you've added lists it any more, so it gets no
      updates. Uninstall it, or add its store again.
    </p>
    <p>
      When two extensions provide the same source, only one of them is used. The other's page lists the source under
      <strong>Provided by other extensions</strong>, with <strong>Use this one</strong> to switch.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="video" title="A video won't play">
    <p>The player's error says what went wrong, and its buttons offer what can help.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>If the source or its server refuses, tap <strong>Open in WebView</strong>, pass the site's check, and tap <strong>Retry</strong>.</li>
      <li>If the video still won't play, try another server or quality, or on Android the other player.</li>
      <li>
        <PlatformLabel platform="Android" lead />If <a href={sitePath("docs/video/#mpv-addon")}>the mpv add-on</a> is missing or out of date, its row under
        <Path>Settings › Players › Anime player</Path> says so and offers the fix.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Task>

  <Task id="background" title="Updates or backups stop in the background">
    <PlatformAnswers>
      <p slot="android">
        Android puts apps to sleep to save battery, and some phone makers are much stricter about it. Under
        <Path>Settings › Advanced</Path>, tap <strong>Disable battery optimization</strong>, then follow the steps for your phone on
        <a href="https://dontkillmyapp.com" {...leavesSite()}>Don't kill my app!</a>
      </p>
      <p slot="linux">
        Updates and backups run only while Katari is open. One missed while it was closed runs when Katari next starts, and quitting
        during one asks first.
      </p>
    </PlatformAnswers>
  </Task>

  <Task id="models" title="Text recognition models don't download" platform="Android">
    <p>
      <strong>Waiting for a non-metered network</strong> means the download was approved for Wi-Fi only. Connect to Wi-Fi, or start it
      again and tick <strong>Allow download over mobile data</strong>. <strong>Not enough storage</strong> needs space freed first. See
      <a href={sitePath("docs/translation/#text-recognition")}>Translation</a>.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="app-update" title="An app update won't install">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <PlatformLabel platform="Android" lead /><strong>This update is signed with a different key than the installed app</strong>: the
        Katari you have was built by someone else, and Android won't replace it.
        <a href={sitePath("docs/backups-and-storage/#backup")}>Back up</a>, uninstall it, install Katari from the
        <a href={sitePath("download/")}>download page</a>, and restore the backup.
      </li>
      <li><strong>GitHub is limiting update checks from this network</strong>: try again later.</li>
      <li>
        <PlatformLabel platform="Android" lead /><strong>There isn't enough free storage to install the update</strong>: free some space
        and try again.
      </li>
      <li>
        <PlatformLabel platform="Linux" lead /><strong>Katari can't replace its AppImage at …</strong>: move the AppImage to a folder you
        can write to, or download the new version from the <a href={sitePath("download/")}>download page</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Callout label="Separate apps">
      <p>
        Katari, Katari FOSS and Katari for Linux are separate apps with separate libraries. Installing one doesn't update or replace
        another; move your library between them with a <a href={sitePath("docs/backups-and-storage/#backup")}>backup</a>.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Task>

  <Task id="report" title="Report a problem">
    <ol>
      <li>
        Under <Path>Settings › Advanced</Path>, select <strong>Share crash logs</strong>. On Android, save or send the file from the share
        sheet; on Linux, choose where to save it.
      </li>
      <li>In <Path>Settings › About</Path>, select <strong>Version</strong> to copy the version details.</li>
      <li>Open an <a href={`${REPOSITORY}/issues`} {...leavesSite()}>issue on GitHub</a> with both, and the steps that lead to the problem.</li>
    </ol>
  </Task>
</GuidePage>
